---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import hello from '../../snippets/hello.ard?raw';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Page title="Get started — Cooper">
  <article class="container article prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Your first terminal interface</p>
    <h1>Start small. Build from here.</h1>
    <p class="lead">Create a Cooper application, describe a view, and mount it. This example uses CUI, Cooper’s experimental component layer.</p>
    <h2>1. Install Ard</h2>
    <p>You’ll need <strong>Ard 0.42.0 or newer</strong> and its Go toolchain. Follow the <a href="https://ard.run">Ard installation guide</a> for your platform. Cooper is actively evolving; API changes are possible.</p>
    <h2>2. Create a project</h2>
    <p>In an empty directory, create an <code>ard.toml</code> file:</p>
    <Code code={'name = "hello-cooper"\nard = ">= 0.42.0"'} filename="ard.toml" label="TOML" />
    <p>Add Cooper to the project:</p>
    <Code code="ard add github.com/akonwi/cooper@latest" filename="Terminal" label="Shell" />
    <h2>3. Describe your interface</h2>
    <p>Save this complete program as <code>main.ard</code>:</p>
    <Code code={hello} filename="main.ard" />
    <p><code>cui::mount(app, view)</code> attaches the view to the application. The renderer owns its retained controls; <code>defer</code> releases the renderer and application when the program exits.</p>
    <h2>4. Run it</h2>
    <Code code="ard run main.ard" filename="Terminal" label="Shell" />
    <p>You’ll see two lines of text in your terminal. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> to quit.</p>
    <aside class="note"><strong>Ready for interaction?</strong> CUI inputs are controlled: keep their value in component state and update it in <code>on_input</code>. The <a href={`${base}guide/#api-shape`}>component guide</a> explains state, props, and callbacks.</aside>
    <h2>Two ways to build</h2>
    <p><strong>CUI</strong> describes views and reconciles them onto retained controls. Use it for typed component props, local state, lifecycle, and keyed lists.</p>
    <p><strong>The imperative API</strong> lets you construct controls once and mutate them directly. It remains available independently of CUI. See the <a href="https://github.com/akonwi/cooper#application-shape">imperative quickstart</a>.</p>
    <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={`${base}guide/`}>Read the guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button secondary" href={`${base}examples/`}>Browse examples</a></div>
  </article>
</Page>
